1
00:00:02,750 --> 00:00:05,900
This is Drizzle. He lives in Gull Harbor, Washington.

2
00:00:05,950 --> 00:00:10,460
Two thousand people, one road out, and one of the most dangerous fault

3
00:00:10,510 --> 00:00:13,580
lines in North America just off the coast.

4
00:00:16,667 --> 00:00:20,477
Drizzle wasn't a prepper. But one fact had stuck with him.

5
00:00:20,937 --> 00:00:25,137
On this coast, if the ground shakes hard, the ocean can arrive in about

6
00:00:25,187 --> 00:00:26,587
fifteen minutes.

7
00:00:29,470 --> 00:00:33,450
At 10:42 on a Tuesday, his coffee started to shiver.

8
00:00:33,990 --> 00:00:35,330
Then the floor moved.

9
00:00:35,755 --> 00:00:37,955
His first instinct was the doorway.

10
00:00:38,295 --> 00:00:42,775
Most people's is. But in a modern house, a doorway is no stronger than

11
00:00:42,825 --> 00:00:46,385
the rest of the wall, and it's exactly where things fall.

12
00:00:46,801 --> 00:00:49,031
So he did what the drills teach.

13
00:00:51,923 --> 00:00:54,043
Drop before the quake drops you.

14
00:00:54,443 --> 00:00:56,523
Cover your head under something sturdy.

15
00:00:56,873 --> 00:01:01,323
Hold on to it, because the table will move, and you need to move with it.

16
00:01:01,751 --> 00:01:04,651
Mags, his neighbor, was already under there.

17
00:01:05,011 --> 00:01:09,561
She'd come over for coffee. She'd done this drill every October for thirty

18
00:01:09,611 --> 00:01:10,081
years.

19
00:01:10,534 --> 00:01:13,374
Ten seconds. Thirty. A minute.

20
00:01:13,924 --> 00:01:17,384
Small quakes end fast. This one kept going.

21
00:01:20,282 --> 00:01:24,152
Four minutes of shaking. That told Drizzle everything.

22
00:01:24,522 --> 00:01:28,822
This was the big one. And the clock in his head started counting down.

23
00:01:29,325 --> 00:01:30,775
Then the lights went out.

24
00:01:32,512 --> 00:01:34,912
The shaking stopped at 10:46.

25
00:01:35,382 --> 00:01:40,072
If the ocean came in fifteen minutes, it would arrive around 11:01.

26
00:01:40,572 --> 00:01:42,582
The walk to high ground took twelve.

27
00:01:45,973 --> 00:01:48,523
Three minutes. No time to pack a life.

28
00:01:48,833 --> 00:01:51,563
No time to look for keys or check the news.

29
00:01:52,016 --> 00:01:54,756
He grabbed his laptop. His photo album.

30
00:01:54,966 --> 00:01:57,906
A plant. Then he put them all down.

31
00:02:00,796 --> 00:02:04,166
The go-bag by the door was packed for exactly this.

32
00:02:04,486 --> 00:02:09,116
Water, a flashlight, a radio, medicine, a warm layer.

33
00:02:09,486 --> 00:02:11,576
One bag. Three seconds.

34
00:02:12,108 --> 00:02:14,658
MAGS: "No siren's coming. We go now."

35
00:02:14,808 --> 00:02:19,298
She was right. With the power out, the sirens might never sound.

36
00:02:19,768 --> 00:02:22,478
On this coast, the shaking is the warning.

37
00:02:22,938 --> 00:02:25,488
Outside, the street had cracked open.

38
00:02:25,888 --> 00:02:28,588
And down at the harbor, the water was leaving.

39
00:02:31,830 --> 00:02:35,550
Boats sat in the mud. A few people walked out to look.

40
00:02:36,070 --> 00:02:38,560
That's the deadliest mistake on the coast.

41
00:02:38,980 --> 00:02:41,840
When the sea pulls back, it comes back bigger.

42
00:02:42,311 --> 00:02:44,481
Harbor Way was already jammed.

43
00:02:44,831 --> 00:02:47,841
One crack in the road can stop a thousand cars.

44
00:02:48,161 --> 00:02:50,201
It can't stop two people on foot.

45
00:02:53,024 --> 00:02:56,414
So they walked. Uphill. Right now.

46
00:02:56,844 --> 00:03:00,714
The evacuation route was point four miles of steep, muddy trail.

47
00:03:00,764 --> 00:03:03,694
Twelve minutes, on a good day.

48
00:03:06,938 --> 00:03:09,328
Halfway up, an aftershock hit.

49
00:03:09,668 --> 00:03:14,388
No table out here. So they got low, covered their heads, and stayed clear

50
00:03:14,438 --> 00:03:15,468
of the trees.

51
00:03:15,894 --> 00:03:19,514
Then Mags' knees gave out. Drizzle didn't argue.

52
00:03:19,964 --> 00:03:22,364
He gave her his shoulder, and they kept moving.

53
00:03:22,794 --> 00:03:24,464
Slower, but moving.

54
00:03:26,796 --> 00:03:31,746
RADIO: "…tsunami warning for the entire Washington coast… move to high ground immediately…"

55
00:03:31,896 --> 00:03:35,506
The official warning came twenty minutes after the shaking started.

56
00:03:36,046 --> 00:03:39,026
Anyone who waited for it was already out of time.

57
00:03:39,504 --> 00:03:41,814
At the switchback, Drizzle looked back.

58
00:03:42,344 --> 00:03:45,004
A gray line stretched across the horizon.

59
00:03:48,039 --> 00:03:51,429
At 11:04, the first wave hit Gull Harbor.

60
00:03:51,849 --> 00:03:53,819
It took the dock. The ferry.

61
00:03:54,149 --> 00:03:55,249
Then Main Street.

62
00:03:58,651 --> 00:04:01,251
And the first wave is rarely the last.

63
00:04:01,611 --> 00:04:04,491
Tsunamis come in sets, for hours.

64
00:04:04,821 --> 00:04:07,561
You don't go back down until officials say it's safe.

65
00:04:08,966 --> 00:04:12,596
The hilltop was cold, crowded, and above the water.

66
00:04:13,076 --> 00:04:14,636
Drizzle checked what he had.

67
00:04:17,537 --> 00:04:21,927
Water first. One gallon per person, per day.

68
00:04:21,977 --> 00:04:23,607
His bag held three days.

69
00:04:26,487 --> 00:04:30,897
Food second. About two thousand calories a day, for three days.

70
00:04:31,217 --> 00:04:35,417
That's the minimum. Out here, they tell you to plan for two weeks.

71
00:04:38,309 --> 00:04:40,379
Drizzle counted what he'd done right.

72
00:04:40,797 --> 00:04:44,737
One. Drop, cover, hold on. Two.

73
00:04:45,107 --> 00:04:47,547
On the coast, the shaking is your warning.

74
00:04:47,767 --> 00:04:52,927
Walk uphill, now. Three. Grab the go-bag, not your stuff.

75
00:04:53,446 --> 00:04:55,906
And four. He'd packed snacks.

76
00:04:56,246 --> 00:04:58,006
Mags took one without a word.

77
00:04:58,494 --> 00:05:00,314
Then the radio spoke again.

78
00:05:00,898 --> 00:05:05,848
RADIO: "…Highway 101 bridge at Gull Creek has collapsed… coastal roads closed…"

79
00:05:05,998 --> 00:05:11,438
The only road out was gone. The whole coast had been hit at once, so help

80
00:05:11,488 --> 00:05:14,858
would come by air and sea, and it would take days.

81
00:05:15,404 --> 00:05:17,954
DRIZZLE: "So… nobody's coming?"

82
00:05:18,104 --> 00:05:20,854
Below them, the water was still rising.

83
00:05:21,334 --> 00:05:26,274
Night was coming. And somewhere down in the dark, a light flickered on

84
00:05:26,324 --> 00:05:27,574
where no one should be.
